A diamond of traits, for the native link's tests (tests/native-link.scala): trait A0 with a method
of its own, trait B0 extending A0, then for each n from 1 to 36 the traits A<n> and B<n>, each
extending A<n-1> with B<n-1>; for each depth the suite links (2 to 20, 28 and 36) a class Leaf<n>
extending A<n> with B<n> and an object Main<n> whose main prints its value. A walk of a class's
parents and traits that goes down every path meets the traits below A<n> once for each path to
them, which doubles with each level: Scala Native 0.5.12's link walks so when it reaches the
allocation of Leaf<n> (`Reach.reachAllocation`): its two links of a build took 16 s at the depth
of 17, 23 s at 18, 45 s at 19 and 106 s at 20, so that past the low twenties it does not end.
teq's link meets each trait once, which gives the same listing (src/native/reach.rs,
`gathered_calls`).

src/          the program: Diamond.scala, and project.scala with the build's directives
nir/          its .nir files, written by the compiler plugin of Scala Native 0.5.12 for Scala 3.8.4
              (scala-cli compile of src/, the files taken from the classes directory)
digests.txt   for each depth, the SHA-256 and the lines of the listing of the link of diamond.Main<n>
              over nir/ and the jars of tests/nir/digests.txt in that file's order, for the target
              x86_64-unknown-linux-musl, threads as the build decides, and whose listing it is:
                scala-native  Scala Native's own: its link's dump (linked.hnir), remade as
                              tests/native-link/README says with the main class diamond.Main<n>,
                              for the depths 2 to 20, where its link ends
                teq           teq's own listing, for the depths 28 and 36: Scala Native's link does
                              not end there, so there is NO ORACLE for them. The digest is what teq
                              printed when the case was added, from the binary whose listings at 2
                              to 20 are Scala Native's; it holds those bytes, it does not prove them.
                              Beside it the suite checks that the listing defines each class and
                              trait of the diamond once and no other of them

The suite links each depth within a bound of 60 seconds. With a release build teq's link takes as
long at every depth as at the smallest, a few seconds on a loaded machine (as long as the link of
tests/nir/fixtures there), where a walk that goes down every path took 198 s at the depth of 28 on
the same machine. src/ is remade by a script outside the tree for those depths; the
teq digests are remade only from a binary whose listings at 2 to 20 match the scala-native digests,
with `teq nir --link diamond.Main<n> <the class path> --triple=x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`.
